The Saga Design Pattern is one of the most important patterns for microservices, especially when you need data consistency across multiple services without using distributed transactions (2PC).

I’ll explain this from fundamentals → architecture → implementation → failure handling → real-world examples, with diagrams and code-style explanations aligned with how it’s asked in senior backend / system design interviews.

---

 1. Why Saga Pattern Exists (The Core Problem)

 Monolith world
- Single database
- ACID transaction
- Easy rollback

BEGIN
  debit account
  create order
  reserve inventory
COMMIT


 Microservices world
- Each service has its own database
- No shared DB
- Distributed transactions (2PC) are avoided because:
  - Poor scalability
  - Tight coupling
  - Blocking
  - Complex failure handling

❌ You cannot do:

BEGIN TRANSACTION across OrderDB + PaymentDB + InventoryDB


👉 Saga Pattern solves this

---

 2. What Is a Saga?

> A Saga is a sequence of local transactions where:
- Each service performs its own transaction
- Publishes an event
- If a step fails → compensating transactions are executed to undo previous steps

 Key idea
✔ Forward actions  
✔ Compensating (undo) actions  
✔ Event-driven orchestration  

---

 3. Core Components of Saga

| Component | Description |
|--------|------------|
| Local Transaction | DB transaction inside a service |
| Event | Notifies next step |
| Compensating Transaction | Reverts a completed step |
| Saga State | Tracks progress |
| Saga Coordinator | Orchestrates or reacts |

---

 4. Two Types of Saga Patterns

 1️⃣ Choreography-based Saga (Event-driven)

Services react to events  
No central coordinator

 2️⃣ Orchestration-based Saga (Central controller)

A Saga Orchestrator decides the flow

---

 5. Choreography-Based Saga (In Depth)

 Flow Example: Order Creation


Order Service
   |
   | OrderCreated
   ↓
Payment Service
   |
   | PaymentCompleted
   ↓
Inventory Service
   |
   | InventoryReserved
   ↓
Order Service (COMPLETED)


 Failure case


Inventory Service fails
   |
   | InventoryFailed
   ↓
Payment Service → RefundPayment
   ↓
Order Service → CancelOrder


 Characteristics

✅ Fully decoupled  
✅ Highly scalable  
❌ Hard to understand flows  
❌ Complex debugging  
❌ Event explosion

---

 Choreography Example (Pseudo Code)

Order Service
java
@Transactional
public void createOrder() {
  orderRepository.save(order);
  eventBus.publish(new OrderCreated(orderId));
}


Payment Service
java
@EventListener
public void handleOrderCreated(OrderCreated event) {
  if (charge()) {
    eventBus.publish(new PaymentSuccess(event.orderId));
  } else {
    eventBus.publish(new PaymentFailed(event.orderId));
  }
}


Compensation
java
@EventListener
public void handleInventoryFailed(InventoryFailed e) {
  refundPayment(e.orderId);
}


