 7. Saga State Management

Saga must track:

| Field | Example |
|----|-------|
| Saga ID | uuid |
| Current Step | PAYMENT |
| Status | IN_PROGRESS |
| Completed Steps | ORDER_CREATED |
| Retry Count | 3 |

Stored in:
- Database
- Kafka compacted topic
- Workflow engine (Camunda, Temporal)

---

 8. Compensation vs Rollback

| Rollback | Compensation |
|--------|-------------|
| Instant | Eventually consistent |
| DB-level | Business logic |
| ACID | Best-effort |

 Example
| Action | Compensation |
|----|----|
| Create Order | Cancel Order |
| Debit Account | Refund |
| Reserve Inventory | Release Inventory |

⚠ Compensation is not always perfect  
(e.g., email sent cannot be undone)

---

 9. Handling Failures & Edge Cases

 1️⃣ Idempotency
Every command/event must be idempotent

java
if(processed(eventId)) return;


---

 2️⃣ Retry Strategy

- Exponential backoff
- Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)

---

 3️⃣ Timeout Handling

If no response in X time → trigger compensation

---

 4️⃣ Partial Failure

Saga can:
- Retry
- Compensate
- Pause for manual intervention

---

 10. Saga vs 2PC (Interview Favorite)

| Aspect | Saga | 2PC |
|----|----|----|
| Scalability | High | Low |
| Coupling | Loose | Tight |
| Blocking | No | Yes |
| Failure handling | Complex | Simple |
| Consistency | Eventual | Strong |

---

 11. When to Use Saga (Very Important)

✅ Use Saga when:
- Distributed data ownership
- Business workflows
- Long-running transactions
- Eventual consistency is acceptable

❌ Avoid Saga when:
- Simple CRUD
- Strong consistency required (bank ledger core)
- Very small systems

---

 12. Real-World Use Cases

- E-commerce checkout
- Travel booking (flight + hotel + cab)
- Subscription management
- Procurement & ERP workflows
- Order-to-Cash pipelines

(Used heavily by Amazon, Uber, Netflix)

